---
// Competitor cost-guide post (issue #148/#603). "procore pricing" queries are
// owned by third-party blogs because Procore quotes on construction volume —
// proof a low-authority domain can rank here. Figures verified 08-10 from the
// intel sweep; re-verify before meaningful edits. Do not invent current quote
// amounts.
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import FAQSection from '../../components/FAQSection.astro';
import BlogPostFooter from '../../components/BlogPostFooter.astro';

const title = 'Procore Pricing 2026: Cost, Plans & Quotes';
const description =
  'Procore pricing is quote-only in 2026. Approximate ACV cost ranges, what subcontractors actually pay, and how the cost compares to a flat rate.';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How much does Procore cost?',
    answer:
      'Procore pricing is quote-only. Quotes are based on your Annual Construction Volume (the dollar value of the work you run) and which product modules you license. Independent analyses converge on roughly 0.1–0.2% of ACV, about $700–$1,000 per $1M of volume: small contractors commonly land at $4,500–$10,000/year, with typical mid-size contracts at $15,000–$80,000/year. Verify with Procore; every quote is negotiated.',
  },
  {
    question: "What are Procore's pricing plans?",
    answer:
      'Procore does not publish named price tiers. The “plan” is a custom bundle of product modules — project management, financials, quality & safety, and so on — quoted against your construction volume. Treat any public dollar figure as someone else’s report of a quote, not a catalog SKU.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does Procore charge per user?',
    answer:
      "No — and Procore says so prominently: unlimited users, unlimited data, no per-seat fees. The cost driver is your construction volume and module bundle instead. That's genuinely better than per-seat pricing for large teams, but it means your software bill grows because your revenue grew — a percentage tax on your growth.",
  },
  {
    question: 'How much do subcontractors pay for Procore?',
    answer:
      "Collaborating is free; running your business isn't. When a GC invites you into their Procore project, you can use it on that project at no charge. Owning your own license is the ACV quote: independent trackers put small contractors at $4,500–$10,000/year before implementation. Most specialty subs keep the free collaborator seat for GC jobs and run bidding, pay apps, and CRM in a system they control.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Is Procore worth it for a small subcontractor?',
    answer:
      "Procore's own reviewers answer this one: the consensus in reviews and contractor forums is that it's overkill below roughly $5M in annual revenue — 'a sledgehammer for smaller projects,' as one puts it, with a reviewer at $8M revenue saying they loved the product but couldn't justify the cost. Add implementation (reported at $50,000+ for larger rollouts, with 2–6 months to full adoption) and the entry math rarely works for a small crew.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Is Procore free for subcontractors?',
    answer:
      "Collaborating is free; running your business isn't. When a GC invites you into their Procore project, you can use it on that project at no charge — worth doing for their RFIs, drawings, and submittals. But that access covers only their jobs, on their terms. Your own bids, pay applications across every GC, change orders, and crew hours still need a system you control.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What does BuildWorkPro cost compared to Procore?',
    answer:
      'BuildWorkPro is $79/month flat ($790/year), published — every feature, unlimited users, 14-day self-serve trial, no demo call. It covers the subcontractor loop Procore prices at enterprise scale: bidding, AIA-style pay applications with schedule of values and retainage, change orders, site logs, time tracking, and CRM.',
  },
];
---

<Layout ogType="article" title={title} description={description}>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20">
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="mb-12">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-3">Blog</p>
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight leading-tight">
          Procore Pricing 2026: Cost, Plans &amp; Quotes
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-400">
          Published August 10, 2026 · Updated September 11, 2026 · 9 min read
        </p>
      </div>

      <div
        class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-brand-600"
      >
        <p>
          Procore pricing is not a number on the website. The pricing page explains the philosophy —
          unlimited users, no hidden fees — then asks you to build a bundle with a construction
          expert. What it does not show is a cost. Enough contractors have shared quotes that
          independent trackers converge on consistent ranges: roughly 0.1–0.2% of Annual
          Construction Volume. Here is what those quotes tend to run, what subcontractors actually
          pay (collaborating is free; owning a license is not), and when the math works — so you
          know the territory before the sales call.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-6 my-8">
          <h2 class="mt-0 text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Key takeaways</h2>
          <ul class="mb-0 mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
            <li>
              Procore pricing is custom as of 2026 — quotes are based on
              <strong>Annual Construction Volume</strong> and which modules you license, not a public
              rate card.
            </li>
            <li>
              Independent analyses put the effective rate at roughly
              <strong>0.1–0.2% of ACV</strong> (about $700–$1,000 per $1M of work). Treat those as approximate,
              independently reported ranges — not a live price list.
            </li>
            <li>
              Small contractors commonly land at <strong>$4,500–$10,000/year</strong>; typical
              contracts run $15,000–$80,000/year, before implementation.
            </li>
            <li>
              Larger rollouts report implementation at $50,000+ and 2–6 months to full adoption.
            </li>
            <li>
              Reviewer consensus: overkill below ~$5M revenue — "a sledgehammer for smaller
              projects."
            </li>
            <li>
              If a GC invites you into their Procore, that's free — and it doesn't run your
              business.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2>How Procore pricing works</h2>
        <p>
          Your quote is a function of two inputs: the annual dollar volume of construction you run
          through the platform, and which modules you license (project management, financials,
          quality &amp; safety, and so on). Procore's defensive copy is accurate — they really don't
          charge per user or per project. But the structure has a consequence worth naming plainly:
          <strong>as your revenue grows, your software bill grows with it</strong>, at roughly a
          tenth to a fifth of a percent. Win $2M more work next year and the renewal quote knows.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is the difference between "Procore cost this year" and "Procore cost over a
          three-year contract." A first-year number in the small-contractor band can still become a
          mid-size bill without you adding users — because volume and the module bundle moved, not
          your seat count. If a competing site lists a single "Procore cost" as if it were a SKU,
          that figure is someone's report of a quote, not a catalog price.
        </p>

        <h2>Procore plans: modules, not a public rate card</h2>
        <p>
          Unlike products that used to print Essential / Advanced / Complete, Procore does not
          publish named pricing plans. The sales motion is: pick a product bundle, disclose your
          construction volume, get a quote. Public marketing still groups the platform into product
          families — project management, financials, quality &amp; safety, and related modules — but
          those names are not a price list. A 2026 custom quote may mix them differently than last
          year's.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Project management</strong> — the operations layer GCs buy Procore for: RFIs, submittals,
            drawings, and coordination at portfolio scale.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Financials</strong> — owner/GC money tools: commitments, invoices, and progress billing
            on <em>their</em> projects. That is not the same loop as a sub building an estimate, sending
            a
            <a href="/blog/aia-pay-application-guide/">pay application</a> against a schedule of values,
            and tracking retainage across every GC.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Quality &amp; safety</strong> — inspections, observations, and related field quality
            workflows. Whether they sit in your quote as core or add-on is a question for the rep.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Ask the rep to list what is in the quote versus what is an add-on — module names are not a
          substitute for a line-item of products. If you only need
          <a href="/features/construction-bidding/">construction bidding software</a> and
          <a href="/features/construction-crm/">construction CRM</a> for the jobs you sell, you are pricing
          a platform built to receive work from many trades, not to run your pipeline.
        </p>

        <h2>The ranges the trackers converge on</h2>
        <p>
          Independent pricing analyses (Projul, CostBench, ScanManifold, and ITQlick) still track
          the same ACV-percentage band. Annual ranges below are approximate and independently
          reported as of August 2026. Verify current pricing with Procore; every quote is
          negotiated.
        </p>
        <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50">
                <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">Contractor size</th>
                <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900"
                  >Reported annual cost</th
                >
                <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">Notes</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Small contractor</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">$4,500–$10,000/yr</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-500">~$375–$835/month effective floor</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Typical contract</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">$15,000–$80,000/yr</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-500">Scales with ACV and module bundle</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700"
                  >Implementation (larger rollouts)</td
                >
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">$50,000+ first year</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-500">Plus 2–6 months to full adoption</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-500">
          Effective monthly column for the small-contractor band is the reported annual range ÷ 12,
          rounded. The 0.1–0.2% ACV rate (about $700–$1,000 per $1M of volume) is the percentage
          independent trackers use to sanity-check a quote — not a published Procore tariff.
        </p>

        <h2>What subcontractors actually pay</h2>
        <p>
          One more number: $0. When a GC invites you into their Procore project, collaborating there
          costs you nothing — use it for their RFIs, drawings, and submittals. It just doesn't run
          <em>your</em> business: your bids across every GC, your pay applications, your crew hours, your
          pipeline. Keeping your own system while collaborating in theirs is the normal setup, and our
          <a href="/compare/procore-alternative/">BuildWorkPro vs Procore comparison</a> covers how they
          coexist.
        </p>
        <p>
          Owning a Procore license is the other bill — the ACV quote in the table above. For a
          specialty sub whose customers are GCs, that license is easy to confuse with the free seat
          you already have. The free seat is the GC's project. The paid seat is a platform priced
          for GCs and owners running portfolios. If your month ends with a pay application against a
          schedule of values, you would be paying a volume tax for coordination workflows you will
          never open — and still stitching
          <a href="/blog/construction-change-order-management/">change orders</a> and retainage in a spreadsheet.
          That is the subcontractor angle on Procore cost: the sticker is only half of it; the other half
          is buying the wrong billing loop.
        </p>

        <h2>What Procore is actually worth it for</h2>
        <p>
          Fairness matters here: Procore is the deepest platform in construction for general
          contractors and owners running portfolios — RFIs and submittals at scale, drawing
          management, BIM coordination, a huge app marketplace. It even courts subcontractors
          directly, with a dedicated landing page quoting capacity and margin gains for large
          specialty firms. If you're a $20M mechanical contractor with a project-engineering
          department, the math can work. The reviewer consensus draws the line lower down: below
          roughly $5M in revenue, you're buying scale you can't use.
        </p>

        <h2>Procore pricing vs BuildWorkPro</h2>
        <p>
          BuildWorkPro publishes its price:
          <a href="/pricing/">$79/month flat, or $790/year</a> — every feature, unlimited users, built
          for the sub-to-GC loop (bids, AIA-style pay applications, change orders, e-signatures, QuickBooks
          sync). There is no quote call because there is nothing to quote, and the 14-day trial needs
          no credit card. At the small-contractor Procore entry of $4,500–$10,000/year, the difference
          funds a crew member's tools, a truck payment, or simply stays margin — before implementation
          on the Procore side.
        </p>
        <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50">
                <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900"></th>
                <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">Procore</th>
                <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">BuildWorkPro</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Pricing model</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Custom quote by ACV &amp; modules</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Published flat rate</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Reported / list price</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">~$4,500–$10,000/yr small-contractor band</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">$79/mo or $790/year</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Users</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Unlimited</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Unlimited</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">How you buy</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Demo and sales quote</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Self-serve, 14-day trial, no card</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Built for</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">GCs &amp; owners</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Trade subs billing GCs</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Progress billing</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">GC/owner financials on their jobs</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">AIA-style pay apps, SOV, retainage</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-slate-700">Implementation</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">$50,000+ reported for larger rollouts</td>
                <td class="px-5 py-3 text-slate-600">Same-day — CSV imports, no fee</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Shopping the other quote-only platform? See
          <a href="/blog/buildertrend-pricing/">Buildertrend pricing and cost</a> — last-published plans,
          the renewal pattern, and the subcontractor fit question. Different buyer, same lesson: if the
          vendor will not print a price, budget the quote <em>and</em> the renewal.
        </p>

        <h2>Questions worth asking before you sign</h2>
        <p>
          A sales-led Procore pricing call is more useful if you arrive with a short list. None of
          these require you to accept the first number:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            What Annual Construction Volume did you use, and what is the renewal if volume stays
            flat versus grows?
          </li>
          <li>Which modules are in this quote, and which are add-ons later?</li>
          <li>
            Is there an onboarding, implementation, or training fee on top of the subscription?
          </li>
          <li>Month-to-month versus annual — and what happens if I cancel mid-term?</li>
          <li>Show me the full-account export (not item-by-item download) before I sign.</li>
          <li>
            If I am a sub already collaborating in GCs' Procore projects for free, which of these
            paid modules will my superintendent actually use?
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-10 text-xs text-slate-400">
        Sources: procore.com pricing page and subcontractor landing page; Projul, CostBench,
        ScanManifold, and ITQlick pricing analyses; G2 and Capterra verified reviews. Figures as of
        August 2026 — independently reported ACV ranges are approximate; every Procore quote is
        negotiated. Verify current pricing with Procore.
      </p>

      <FAQSection bare class="mt-16" faqs={faqs} heading="Procore pricing FAQ" />

      <BlogPostFooter
        headline={title}
        description={description}
        datePublished="2026-08-10"
        dateModified="2026-09-11"
        related={[
          {
            href: '/compare/procore-alternative/',
            title: 'BuildWorkPro vs Procore',
            description:
              'The full comparison: where Procore genuinely wins, and what subs run instead.',
          },
          {
            href: '/blog/buildertrend-pricing/',
            title: 'Buildertrend Pricing 2026: Cost, Plans & Quotes',
            description:
              'The other gated-pricing giant, decoded — last-published plans and the hike pattern.',
          },
          {
            href: '/pricing/',
            title: 'BuildWorkPro Pricing',
            description: '$79/month flat, published. The honest math for a 5-person crew.',
          },
        ]}
      />
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
